System Documentation: HFS-ai Kiosk Front-End
Version: 1.0
Last Updated: June 7, 2025
This document outlines the architecture, dependencies, configuration, and deployment procedures for the HFS-ai Kiosk application.
1.0 Overview
The HFS-ai Kiosk is a self-contained, browser-based chat interface designed to provide a front-end for a local Large Language Model (LLM). It features a clean, minimal UI, supports both document and image uploads for multimodal context, and is built to be ephemeral, ensuring no conversation data persists between sessions.
The system is architected as a set of containerized services that communicate over a private network, ensuring the backend models are not directly exposed.
2.0 System Architecture
The application consists of three core, independent services running in Docker containers:
1.	Kiosk Front-End (kiosk-app): A Streamlit application that serves the user-facing web interface. This is the only service that exposes a port to the local network.
2.	LLM Backend (llm-server): A llama.cpp instance running in its OpenAI-compatible server mode. It performs all language and vision processing. It does not expose a port to the local network.
3.	Document Parser (tika-server): An Apache Tika server instance. It is responsible for text extraction and Optical Character Recognition (OCR) from uploaded documents. It does not expose a port to the local network.
These three containers communicate exclusively over a private Docker bridge network named llm-net.
3.0 Core Components & Dependencies
3.1 Kiosk Front-End (kiosk-app)
•	Technology: Python 3.11 / Streamlit
•	Container Name: kiosk-app
•	Exposed Port: 8501
•	Primary Dependencies (Python):
o	streamlit: The core web framework.
o	openai: The client library used to communicate with the llama.cpp server's API.
o	tika: The client library used to communicate with the tika-server.
o	requests: Used for performing health checks on the backend services.
o	pytz: Used for providing timezone-aware timestamps in the system prompt.
3.2 LLM Backend (llm-server)
•	Technology: llama.cpp
•	Container Name: llm-server
•	Network Address: http://llm-server:8080
•	Key Requirement: Must be launched with the --host 0.0.0.0 flag to be reachable by other containers on the network. Must be connected to the llm-net network.
3.3 Document Parser (tika-server)
•	Technology: Apache Tika (Java)
•	Container Name: tika-server
•	Network Address: http://tika-server:9998
•	Key Requirement: Must be connected to the llm-net network.
4.0 Project Files
The project directory (/home/eko/kiosk-llm) contains the following essential files:
•	kiosk_app.py: The main Python application script containing all UI and business logic.
•	Dockerfile: Defines the build instructions for the kiosk-app container image.
•	docker-compose.yml: A Docker Compose file used to manage the lifecycle (build, start, stop) of the kiosk-app container.
•	requirements.txt: A text file listing the specific Python libraries required by the application.
•	.dockerignore: Specifies files and directories to exclude from the Docker build context to improve build speed and security.
5.0 Setup & Deployment Procedure
To deploy the system from scratch, follow these steps in order on the host machine.
5.1 Prerequisites
•	Docker and Docker Compose must be installed.
•	For GPU acceleration, the NVIDIA Container Toolkit must be installed.
•	The user running the commands must have permissions to manage Docker containers.
5.2 Step 1: Create the Private Network
This command only needs to be run once. If the network already exists, it will return a harmless error.
docker network create llm-net
